 that you can  support democracy  separately from your own political views  um this in many countries shouldn't be  a a partisan issue um  and uh what citizens can do i think is  think about  how their actions are both  interpreted as defending democracy in  their country if that's something  they're interested in doing  and something that can be partisan so  there's a range of actions that citizens  can take  to try to support democracy i have to  say that  globally around the world sometimes that  means accepting loss  in an election uh peacefully that's what  democracy is based on and everybody does  have to take  um a time um take a turn sitting  um losing an election and it's  unpleasant right it's unpleasant for  everyone who's ever experienced that i  think around the world  but it's unpleasant when you fear  that the party in power is going to take  away democracy and this will be your  last opportunity to fight for it so i  just encourage the separation of those  two things  yeah thank you for clarifying the  differences between the two we have  a question from marco pom and he asked  how has political advertisement on the  internet with the help of  micro targeting influence elections  that's an interesting question so  there's a version of it that's very  benign  and micro targeting is just more  efficient for political campaigns to  speak to voters about issues they care  about  um if i look at that from the  perspective of  my interest in election manipulation and  election fraud  it's very hard to see in micro targeting  to see disinformation and who the  disinformation might be affecting  in real time such that you can do  something to  defend against that type of  disinformation so for example  a common voter suppression tactic it's  not new  um but bringing micro targeting into it  makes it i think more pernicious which  is  uh when people are told that their no  their polling station has moved when it  has not in fact moved or that their  method of voting that they were planning  to use is no longer a  method of voting that's allowed when  that information is false  it's called voter suppression and it can  be ver it can be much harder to document  that it's happening  call it out and do something about it if  the micro targeting makes it harder to  sort of see all of the ways in which  that disinformation is being  sent out to individual citizens so then  why are all these steps for democracy  backsliding  have been either gradual or are hard to  detect like you're saying what are those  factors  is it because there's no real time to  document that even if  the same voters when they go to the  polls they can record with their phones  and see that they're being suppressed or  places are being shut down yeah  so the so it's very tricky right there  there's cases all around the world of um  disinformation about election  manipulation too right so sometimes  people  um will try to accuse um the other side  of manipulation when they feel like  they're when they feel like they're  losing  and they might just be losing right this  is a very common tactic for losers  or candidates who are losing so um some  people think that the sort of  crowdsourcing  of election protection and the ability  of citizens to record problems with  elections  is going to be a big deterrent um i  think it's possible that that can help  in some cases  i think it's important to note that  there's there's two things going on  there one is that  um if it's just self-reported uh we may  be missing some of the most important  places in which election manipulation is  happening  without a systematic systematic effort  to document it  and if if there's a number of other  things going on as well  it could just be hard to know when some  of those videos are manipulated right  we're getting into the world of deep  fakes we're getting into  technological innovation in which lots  of people can come up with  uh really strange but sometimes  effective ways to try to make their  political opponents look bad and  sometimes that's by  falsifying charges of election  manipulation or  confusing the irregularities that happen  in any election that are not  that are not necessarily a partisan  trying to sort of cast those as as  partisan yeah well i definitely admire  the work that the international election  observance do because  it's definitely uh tricky and we  are getting closer to the end of our  facebook live  uh and i just i would like to squeeze in  one more question as if possible and  his his name is michael erdermann and he  asked when might the level of non-voters  factor into a democracy  so i am a person who thinks that  non-participation  it can be a democratic act for a voter  so some countries have compulsory voting  um then i think it's fine for citizens  to spoil their ballots there's a good  way for  um it's it's part of democracy for  citizens to be able to communicate their  dissatisfaction with all of the options  um and that that's one way to do it um i  do think that  um what we often see is that young  people  um and certain groups within a society  um choose not to participate because  um they don't um feel as though the  process is working for them or something  like that  um and i do think that at least from my  perspective  political participation across all  sectors of society is important  so ranging from young people to old  people but also different demographic  groups  that's an important thing for a country  to have if it is calling itself a  democracy  and so when a lot of people disengage  from the process it gives those who stay  more power um and so that that in itself  is i think a really  uh important thing to acknowledge when  people are turning away from the process  um of course there's something called  election boycotts where parties say this  process is so bad  that our party is refusing to  participate and those can also be very  powerful at  delegitimizing an electoral process when  it's deserved  so so far we've learned what's the role  of an international  observant for elections is about  democracy processes through electing  and even voting or not voting  and as well the different tactics that  many governments might use  through elections to continue to stay in  power  so before we leave this discussion do  you have  anything else that you would like to add  susan especially  uh how the feedback has been uh on your  publication perhaps  yeah i think that um it's been just a  delight and an honor to engage with  people from uh  you know outside of my own field and  outside of political science  about this topic i do think that  there's a lot of changes in the  international system including  including increasing power of russia and  china um  the us turning away from what has been a  global leadership role that has often  been problematic but has been  generally pro-democracy um these things  are  are important global shifts and they  have repercussions in many countries  throughout the world and so  i'm not trying to say that um one is bad  and the other is good  far from it i just think that they're  consequential changes and it will be  interesting to see  uh how they play out in the coming years  well we look forward to your thoughts as  well as all of these things continue to  move  i would like to thank everyone for  joining us today  my name is val from the science magazine  media office team  and you can find susan's latest review  in this post i just clicked on the link  and you can see  further thoughts on susan's about the  democratic backsliding  so thank you everyone so much again  thank you val thank you thank you susan
 

